## 2. Curvature Decomposition

Let `theta` span the vertical `(1,0)` cotangent line and let `H` be the
horizontal complex rank-two distribution with positive transverse form
`omega_B`. Every endomorphism-valued `(1,1)` curvature decomposes uniquely as

\[
 F=F_B+\theta\wedge\bar a+a\wedge\bar\theta
   +f\,\theta\wedge\bar\theta,                          \tag{3}
\]

where `F_B` is horizontal, `a` is a horizontal `(1,0)` form and `f` is an
endomorphism.

Wedge (3) with `omega_B`. Components with distinct vertical degree cannot
cancel, so (1) gives

\[
 F_B\wedge\omega_B=0,\quad
 a\wedge\omega_B=0,\quad
 \bar a\wedge\omega_B=0,\quad
 f\,\omega_B=0.                                        \tag{4}
\]

On a real four-dimensional symplectic vector space, the Lefschetz map

\[
 L:\Lambda^1H^*\longrightarrow\Lambda^3H^*,
 \qquad b\longmapsto b\wedge\omega_B                    \tag{5}
\]

is an isomorphism. Positivity also makes `omega_B` nonzero. Equations (4)
therefore force

\[
 a=0,\qquad f=0,
\]

and `F=F_B` is horizontal and primitive.

## 3. The Third-Chern Obstruction

